 now let's get into why  disney's darth vader works so rogue one  was the film that i think slowly started  to lay the groundwork that disney were  upping the ante when it came to the dark  lord of the sith  when we first meet him on mustafar he's  chilling like a villain in his fortress  waiting for the arrival of orson krennic  we're introduced to him in his back to  tank om severed with smoke masking what  he really looks like from here he  travels to meet the imperial and rather  than putting the focus solely on the  director we see how his shadow looms  over him contrasting the black between  his white uniform shadows have been used  throughout the franchise to show  superiority and it's popped up in a lot  of ship scenes in the saga  rally is it used with a person though  and it instantly implies how  intimidating vader is we leave the  camera on him as he walks up to krennic  and we see how he makes him appear much  smaller in stature they did similar  things with bane and ben mendelsohn and  they maybe maybe give the guy a box or  something now though i think him  dropping puns is a bit out of character  i'll let it slide for the meaning behind  this moment krennic is asking if he's  still in charge and vader just wants him  to shut the f up unlike vader krennic  isn't really a likable villain and  because of this you really side with him  telling him to stop talking now this  entrance and how unstoppable it feels is  murdered later on and during the film's  climax we get the death star plan sent  up to the rebel forces and they even  trick us into thinking this is somewhat  of a triumphant moment through the music  used before vader's star destroyer comes  in out of the blue  all ships prepare for jump to hyperspace  using the star destroyer as a battering  ram he mercilessly ploughs through the  rebel ships which look minuscule in  comparison further alluding to this idea  that vader is a powerful giant in  comparison to everyone else even his  soldiers are afraid of him and we see  this in the moment that an imperial  informs him of what's going on  but has received transmissions from the  surface  the air aborting  just looking at this guy's reaction he  breathes a subtle sigh of relief and we  linger on him for just a second to  enforce the idea that everyone is afraid  of vader from here we get one of the  best scenes in star wars and it had my  jaw on the floor the first time i  watched it  [Music]  there's no debating that this is a  standout in the saga and it's one of the  only times that we've ever seen vader  completely off the leash mercilessly  plowing through people  the original trilogy lightsaber fights  are often criticized as being slow and  lumbering and this is why the prequels  did the complete opposite when it came  to their duels however this feels like  an amazing middle ground that doesn't  have the same twirling styles of those  films but it also retains the ferocity  of them  [Music]  the creative team took a simple  environment like a corridor and showed  metaphorically what would happen if  people were trapped in one with a wild  bear having him standing in the darkness  where we just hear his breathing but  can't see him is an incredible way to  introduce him and it's only as his red  lightsaber sparks up that we do finally  see him the guy cuts through them like a  hot lightsaber through butter and it's  one hell of a way to set up the events  of a new hope this movie instantly makes  you want to jump over to that film and i  think it's purely down to how well vader  leaves his mark now another big reason  that vader is so intimidating in the  hands of disney is because they've made  him look like a horror villain directors  doing this is something that we've  discussed in a lot of our videos  recently and it was a technique that  matt reeves used for his version of  batman reeves had the character appear  from the shadows and slowly he walked  towards his prey unshakable and  completely focused this is a motif that  has been used in characters like the  terminator jason voorhees it follows and  michael myers they just slowly walk  towards their target because they don't  need to run they will still catch up to  their victim and this is something that  is seen in vader now if you play jedi  fall in order then you'll know he makes  a major impact in that towards the end  of the game introduced again by his  breathing it completely shakes the  player and is another standout moment  i've carried so much hate for you  vader chases you through fortress  inquisitorius but he never runs he  simply just strolls towards you however  he is a one hit one kill villain that  will instantly end your life if he  manages to catch up to you you aren't  fighting against him you're doing  whatever you can to get away and it was  a smart move by the creative team to  make him the only character that you  can't actually fight people who've  played the game now have the dread in  their mind that he is someone you have  to get away from which translates into  other media now whereas in the case of  the other characters that adopt this  slow pace towards their target they give  vader a practical reason for doing this  in obi-wan kenobi we see him suiting up  and at one point catches synthetic legs  being put on this is the reason he can't  run and is held back but even being a  quadruple amputee he's still the most  powerful character in the show even when  we first see vader in the series it's  his eyes shooting open like a demon  awakening from a dream the guy is  terrifying and disney wants you to think  he's a frankenstein's monstrous creature  of the night this is murder in his  entrance later on which follows a  similar play to rogue one first we hear  his breathing then we get his looming  shadow on the wall before his breathing  is juxtaposed with obi-wan's  whereas ben panics vader's breathing is  slow and steady and at this point they  took it to a place and never thought  they'd go with disney pulling a father  out he chokes him to death in the air  before snapping his son's neck with a  twist of his hands used to be that we'd  only ever see him killing his own men or  the rebels but here they've upped it and  have him brutally murdering civilians  even third sister is shocked and it  perfectly encapsulates how hard the  studio is going with the villain i would  never have expected this from the  character and even comparing him to the  original trilogy he seems sanitized in  that he starts dragging people through  the streets trying to draw obi-wan out  to his compassion and basically just  all over the shop now in the sand i  don't like sand  sorry in the sand amongst the dunes he  sparks a biseba and this angle is also  something that we've seen used before  this is supposed to mirror the moments  before he struck down the younglings in  the jedi temple and he stands there  deliberate unflinching whilst obi-wan  flees vader at this point reveals he  doesn't want to kill obi-wan he just  wants to make him suffer and as we know  fear is the path to the dark side  fear leads to anger anger leads to hate  hate leads to suffering  now he very much just wants to keep him  alive so that he can feel the torment  that he has this is also why he burns  him beta lets him get away because he  wants to prolong his agony and it's  gonna be interesting to see what they do  with the rest of the series and what  they've also done with disney is that  they've managed to hint to his humanity  too which is ultimately what makes him  help his son at the end of return of the  jedi the final season of the clone wars  ended with his apprentice ahsoka evading  the empire's forces after order 66. she  dumped her lightsaber and vader returned  to look for her along with his army of  probe droids and stormtroopers and  amongst the snow he managed to find her  blade very much connects him to his life  before and though he doesn't say  anything the creative team do a lot with  this moment vader due to his mask is  someone who it's very difficult to get  emotion out of however this final shot  is laced with a lot of sadness regret  and also wonder even though he can't see  his face it sets up so much of what's to  come and perfectly lays the groundwork  for the ending and return of the jedi  it's another reason that i think disney  even with their faults have nailed  probably the most important character in  the entire franchise they've got how  intimidating he is of the brutality in  him but also managed to keep the  humanity that ends up turning him around  overall i'm really feeling what they're  doing with him  just just don't let me down guys yeah  at this point the season still has three  episodes left so  apologies if it turns out to be crap now  obviously i'd love to hear whether you  agree whether you disagree and just what  you think we're running a competition  right now and giving away three copies  of the batman on the 15th of june and  all you have to do to be on the chance  of winning is like the video make sure  you subscribe with notifications on and  drop a comment below with your thoughts  on the character we pick the comments at  random at the end of the month and the  winners of the last one are on screen  right now so if that's you then message  me on twitter at heavyspoilers if you  want some else to watch then make sure  you check out our breakdown of episode 3  which will be linked on screen right now  if not we've got another video there  just in case but hopefully i see over  there right after this without the way  thanks for sticking through this i've  been paul hope to see you next time take  care peace

